cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "The Truth Was Never Gone: Perfect Aliasing in Compliant-Context Truth Probes"
abstract: >
  Linear truth probes transfer well to instructed liars, yet degrade, and in the
  most-cited case fall below chance, against models trained under reward. That
  contrast is usually read representationally: the trained deceiver is said to
  hide or stop representing what it knows. We show the inference does not follow,
  because the probes it rests on are unidentified. In the compliant ("ally")
  contexts where such probes are trained, the model's belief, its intended action
  and its emitted token are perfectly aliased, so nothing distinguishes a truth
  probe from an action probe, and inversion on deceptive contexts is precisely
  what an action probe must do. On compliant data the truth and action labels are
  identical, so the "truth probe" and the action probe are one fitted probe scored
  against opposite labels, and action/ally = 1 - truth/ally at every layer of
  every model tested. Breaking the collinearity with a randomized-codebook task
  and fitting on mixed compliant and deceptive contexts recovers the bit: on the
  same activations at the same layer of a reward-trained model that lies on 100%
  of deceptive trials, the conventional probe reads 0.006 +/- 0.005 across three
  training seeds while the identified probe reads 1.000 with zero seed variance.
